{%- comment -%}
  Snippet: carousel

  A horizontally scrolling rail built on CSS scroll-snap. No library, no JS
  required to scroll — touch, trackpad, shift+wheel and keyboard all drive the
  native scroll container. The arrow buttons are pure enhancement: they ship
  hidden and carousel.js only reveals them on pointer-capable desktop widths
  where the rail actually overflows, so nothing dead renders when JS fails.

  Usage:
    {%- capture rail_items -%}
      {%- for product in products -%}
        <div class="snap-start shrink-0 w-[42%] sm:w-[30%] md:w-[22%]">
          {%- render 'product-card', product: product -%}
        </div>
      {%- endfor -%}
    {%- endcapture -%}
    {%- render 'carousel', items: rail_items, label: 'Best sellers' -%}

  Parameters:
    items — pre-rendered HTML string of slides (required). Each slide must carry
            its own `snap-start shrink-0 w-[..]` classes, because product cards
            and category tiles want different widths.
    label — accessible name for the scroll region (required).

  Slide widths are deliberately a hair under 1/n of the rail (42/30/22%) so the
  next card is always clipped at the right edge. That peek is the only cue a
  shopper gets that the row scrolls, and it is what keeps the row from reading
  as a stunted grid.
{%- endcomment -%}

<div class="relative" data-carousel>

  <button
    type="button"
    data-carousel-prev
    aria-label="Scroll {{ label | escape }} left"
    class="hidden absolute left-0 top-1/3 z-20 w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-cream/90 hover:bg-cream border border-deep/15 text-deep items-center justify-center transition-colors"
  >
    <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true">
      <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M15 19l-7-7 7-7" />
    </svg>
  </button>

  {%- comment -%}
    tabindex="0" makes the rail reachable for arrow-key scrolling: browsers only
    auto-focus scroll containers that have no focusable children, and this one is
    full of product links. It is a stop, not a trap — Tab moves straight on into
    the cards.

    The scrollbar is suppressed three ways (Firefox, legacy Edge, WebKit) while
    the element stays a real scroll container, so assistive tech and keyboards
    still see it.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div
    data-carousel-rail
    tabindex="0"
    role="group"
    aria-label="{{ label | escape }}"
    class="flex gap-3 lg:gap-4 overflow-x-auto overscroll-x-contain snap-x snap-mandatory pb-2 [scrollbar-width:none] [-ms-overflow-style:none] [&::-webkit-scrollbar]:hidden"
  >
    {{ items }}
  </div>

  <button
    type="button"
    data-carousel-next
    aria-label="Scroll {{ label | escape }} right"
    class="hidden absolute right-0 top-1/3 z-20 w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-cream/90 hover:bg-cream border border-deep/15 text-deep items-center justify-center transition-colors"
  >
    <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true">
      <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M9 5l7 7-7 7" />
    </svg>
  </button>

</div>

<script type="module" src="{{ 'carousel.js' | asset_url }}"></script>
